<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  UnfoldMyMac ships with the Hardened Runtime and NO entitlements.

  Empty rather than absent, deliberately. An explicit empty set is a reviewed artifact:
  sign_release.sh asserts the embedded entitlements match this file, so granting a capability
  means editing a file someone reads, not passing an extra codesign flag. It also guarantees
  com.apple.security.get-task-allow is absent, the most common cause of an Invalid
  notarization verdict.

  App Sandbox is OFF and must stay off. LidSensor.swift opens a raw IOHIDDevice (vendor 0x05AC,
  product 0x8104) and reads feature report 1. No sandbox entitlement grants an undocumented
  Apple HID sensor, so the sandbox would break the app's defining feature. The app also writes
  ~/.codex/hooks.json and reads ~/.claude/projects. Sandbox only entitlements such as
  com.apple.security.network.client are therefore meaningless here.

  Considered and rejected:

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit, and allow-unsigned-executable-memory.
  ShaderLibraryCache calls MTLDevice.makeLibrary(source:options:) at runtime. That is not JIT in
  this process: Metal hands the MSL text to the system's out of process compiler over XPC and
  gets back GPU code, which never becomes executable CPU pages in our address space. No W^X
  mapping is created, so neither entitlement is required. This is verified rather than assumed:
  sign_release.sh runs the shader-check and probe commands against the hardened bundle before
  notarization, and shader-check is exactly the path a missing allow-jit would break.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation.
  makeLibrary(URL:) loads Shaders/Compiled/<digest>.metallib. A metallib is an MTLB container,
  not a dyld image, so library validation never sees it. The app loads no third party dylibs and
  hosts no plug-ins.

  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events.
  The app sends no Apple Events. The Finder AppleScript in package_dmg.sh is run by the
  operator's terminal, so it needs Automation permission for that terminal, never an entitlement
  on UnfoldMyMac.

  allow-dyld-environment-variables, cs.debugger, device.camera, device.audio-input and the
  personal-information family are all unused.

  ScreenCaptureKit, NSWorkspace.setDesktopImageURL, NSStatusItem, NSOpenPanel, outbound HTTPS,
  and reading or writing ~/.claude and ~/.codex are gated by TCC or by plain POSIX permissions
  outside the sandbox. None of them is entitlement gated.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
